Yes, I'm familiar with the Push_swap project! It's a common algorithmic project that tests your understanding of sorting algorithms and stack operations. The goal is to sort a stack of numbers using a limited set of operations while trying to use the minimum number of moves possible.

The allowed operations are typically:
```
sa: swap a - swap the first 2 elements at the top of stack a
sb: swap b - swap the first 2 elements at the top of stack b
ss: sa and sb at the same time
pa: push a - take the first element at the top of b and put it at the top of a
pb: push b - take the first element at the top of a and put it at the top of b
ra: rotate a - shift up all elements of stack a by 1
rb: rotate b - shift up all elements of stack b by 1
rr: ra and rb at the same time
rra: reverse rotate a - shift down all elements of stack a by 1
rrb: reverse rotate b - shift down all elements of stack b by 1
rrr: rra and rrb at the same time
```

